//! Long operations, made interruptible.
//!
//! Two movers: `mem`'s two byte loops with a cadence around them. Every
//! `PREEMPT_STRIDE` bytes the operation stops and asks the machine whether it
//! wants anything (`irq.preempt_point`). Everything that copies or clears in
//! bulk goes through these (the loader's segment placement, at boot and at
//! `process_create`), so the audit sentence for a long copy is written once,
//! here.
//!
//! The points are not in `mem` beside the loops they drive because of an import
//! cycle. A point delivers, so it needs `irq.deliver_line`, and delivery
//! reaches down: `deliver_line` -> `on_timer_interrupt` -> `expire_timers` ->
//! `fire_timer` -> `refs.notify_ready` -> `wake_one_waiter` ->
//! `deliver_attachment` -> `process.copy_out` -> `mem.copy_bytes`. A point in
//! the byte loop would make `mem` import `irq`. A `FuncPointer` hook installed
//! at boot cannot break the cycle either: a static initializer must be a
//! compile-time constant, so the hook cannot be declared.
//!
//! The byte loop exists once (`mem`), the point exists once (`irq`), and the
//! cadence joining them exists once (here). A call site chooses an altitude,
//! not a policy: `mem`'s loops are the primitive, these are the operation.
//!
//! A bulk copy issued from below this module cannot take a point. That covers
//! `process.copy_out`/`copy_in`, which move records bounded by `WaitBuffer`
//! (under a single stride, and inert under `IN_DELIVERY` anyway). A move that
//! needed a point would need a pointed sibling of the door above this module.
//! `loader.place_image` sits above this module and calls `long_copy` directly.

import kcore.mem.{copy_bytes, zero_bytes}
import kcore.irq.{preempt_point}

/// How many bytes a long operation moves between preemption points.
///
/// A point costs an interrupt-controller round trip, so polling per byte would
/// multiply a large copy by that cost. A stride's worth of byte moves bounds
/// the masked latency, and one poll per stride adds little to the copy. Both
/// bounds are loose; the stride is meant to be re-measured.
public(package) static PREEMPT_STRIDE: UInt = 4096

/// Copy `len` bytes from `src` to `dst`, stopping to serve the machine every
/// `PREEMPT_STRIDE` bytes.
///
/// The point is after each chunk. Partially-written destination bytes are no
/// IRQ-read state (nothing on the interrupt path reads the region being
/// filled), so between two chunks there is no in-flight invariant for a
/// delivery to land inside. The caller still owes the same argument for its own
/// state: a caller holding a half-built structure the IRQ path can see must
/// finish it before it copies.
public func long_copy(dst: UInt, src: UInt, len: UInt) unsafe {
    var done: UInt = 0
    while done < len {
        var chunk = len - done
        if chunk > PREEMPT_STRIDE {
            chunk = PREEMPT_STRIDE
        }
        copy_bytes(dst + done, src + done, chunk)
        done = done + chunk
        preempt_point()
    }
}

/// Clear `len` bytes at `dst`, stopping to serve the machine every
/// `PREEMPT_STRIDE` bytes.
///
/// Same argument as `long_copy`'s. A root image's `.bss` is the largest single
/// masked window the kernel has.
public func long_zero(dst: UInt, len: UInt) unsafe {
    var done: UInt = 0
    while done < len {
        var chunk = len - done
        if chunk > PREEMPT_STRIDE {
            chunk = PREEMPT_STRIDE
        }
        zero_bytes(dst + done, chunk)
        done = done + chunk
        preempt_point()
    }
}
